The frame dump says which piece of a texture each quad shows.

The interface of the original samples no picture from a file: everything
under `Menu/HUD/Texture/` is packed into `Menu/Atlas/MemeAtlas_010.dds` and
`_020.dds`, and `Menu/Atlas/MemeAtlas.tai` is the index — one line per
original file with its page and its rectangle in UV units. So a call's
texture coordinates **are** the name of what it drew, and the dump was
printing only the first vertex's pair.

This adds two things to the geometry line: `uv=[u,v,w,h]` for the whole call,
and, beside every four-vertex rectangle the batch already lists, the piece of
texture that rectangle shows — `x,y,w,h@u,v,w,h`. `tools/hud_atlas.py` looks
them up in the atlas index and prints the file name.

Where the coordinate sits is not read off the FVF: the interface draws with
one layout only — stride 44, eleven floats, `x y z  r g b a  u0 v0  u1 v1` —
and for any other stride the coordinate is not printed rather than guessed
at. That the pair at 7 and 8 is the atlas coordinate is measured, not
assumed: it lands on an entry of `MemeAtlas.tai` to four decimal places
(docs/research/03-frame-dump.md).

Applies to `reference/mtld3d` after `mtld3d_frame_dump_geom.patch`, whose
`quads=[]` list it extends; `mtld3d_file_trigger.patch` and
`mtld3d_frame_dump_constants.patch` are independent of both.

